<body>
<h1>Unit 17: Lenses</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">Lenses bend light to make images, and the thin-lens equation is the workhorse of the entire unit. What separates school physics from optical engineering is the list of assumptions that equation hides: thinness, paraxial rays, and a single refractive index.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The thin-lens equation is 1/f = 1/v - 1/u under the usual sign convention, and the lensmaker's equation gives 1/f = (n - 1)(1/R1 - 1/R2) for a lens in air.</li><li>The power of a lens is P = 1/f in dioptres; for a lens immersed in a medium of index n_m it becomes (n_l/n_m - 1)(1/R1 - 1/R2).</li><li>A converging lens is thicker at the centre and a diverging lens is thinner at the centre; for a real object a diverging lens always gives a virtual, upright, diminished image.</li><li>In contact, P = P1 + P2; separated by a distance d, the equivalent power is 1/F = 1/f1 + 1/f2 - d/(f1 f2).</li><li>The human eye is a roughly 17 mm lens with a focal length of about 2.3 cm; accommodation works by changing the lens curvature so the image always lands on the retina.</li><li>Magnification m = v/u, and a real image is inverted and can be projected on a screen.</li><li>A lens forms a real or virtual image depending on where the object sits relative to the focal length, and the thin-lens treatment hides the thick-lens corrections and aberrations that real objectives need.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The thin-lens equation requires a lens thin compared with its focal length and paraxial rays; real camera and telescope lenses are thick assemblies whose elements cancel aberrations.</li><li>Chromatic aberration means f depends on wavelength, so a single glass lens focuses blue and red at different distances; an achromatic doublet combines glasses of different dispersion to cancel it.</li><li>Spherical aberration grows with aperture, so stopping down a camera trades brightness for sharpness - until diffraction takes over.</li><li>Depth of field depends on aperture and focal length as well as on the sensor, so it is a property of the optical system, not of the camera body.</li><li>The lensmaker's equation assumes a thin lens in air; a thick or multi-element system is described by its principal planes and effective focal length instead.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Spherical lenses, angular magnification</li><li>Lens maker's formula</li><li>Power of a lens</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A converging lens always forms a real image.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The position of the object decides the case. Inside the focal length the image is virtual, upright and magnified - the image in a magnifying glass, and the near point of a relaxed eye.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For a converging lens: object beyond 2f gives a real, inverted, diminished image between f and 2f; at 2f the image is at 2f and the same size; between f and 2f the image is real, inverted and magnified beyond 2f; at f the image is at infinity; inside f the image is virtual, upright and magnified, on the same side as the object.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A lens's power depends on the glass it is made of and on how fast light travels in it.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The power of a lens in air depends only on its focal length, and the focal length is fixed by the geometry and by the refractive index relative to the surroundings. Speed alone does not set the power.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> P = 1/f in dioptres, with f in metres. From the lens-maker's formula, 1/f = (n - 1)(1/R1 - 1/R2) in air, so the power is set by the relative refractive index n_medium and the curvatures. A lens of the same shape but made of a denser glass has a shorter focal length and a greater power.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Two thin lenses in contact can be replaced by a single lens whose power is the sum of their powers.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That is true only for thin lenses in contact, where the separation is negligible compared with the focal lengths and the lenses can be treated as a single element.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> In contact, P = P1 + P2. Separated by a distance d, the equivalent power is P = P1 + P2 - d P1 P2, and the separation term is exactly why telephoto and zoom combinations are designed with the spacing as a variable.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A converging lens of focal length 20 cm forms a real image of an object placed 60 cm from the lens. Find the image distance and the magnification, and state the power of the lens.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> 1/v = 1/f + 1/u with u = 60 cm and f = 20 cm gives 1/v = 1/20 - 1/60 = (3 - 1)/60 = 2/60, so v = 30 cm. The image is real and inverted, 30 cm from the lens. Magnification m = v/u = 30/60 = 0.5, so the image is half the object's size. Power P = 1/f = 1/0.20 = 5 D.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the lens-maker's formula for a thin lens in air, 1/f = (n - 1)(1/R1 - 1/R2), from the refraction at the two spherical surfaces.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> For the first surface, refraction in a spherical surface gives n2/v1 - n1/u = (n2 - n1)/R1 with n1 = 1 and n2 = n. For the second surface, n/v - n/v1 = (1 - n)/R2, and adding the two equations eliminates the intermediate distance v1: n/v - 1/u = (n - 1)(1/R1 - 1/R2). For a thin lens the object and image distances are measured from the same plane, so 1/v - 1/u = 1/f, which identifies the lens-maker's formula. The sign convention (Cartesian, with light travelling in the positive direction) decides the signs of R1 and R2: a surface convex to the incident light has R1 positive.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A photographer stops the lens down from f/2.8 to f/16 to increase depth of field. Explain what is gained and what is lost, and identify the physical limit that eventually stops the gain.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Stopping down reduces the diameter of the beam admitted by the lens, and a narrower beam makes the range of object distances that map to an acceptable circle of confusion smaller, so the depth of field grows - the gain. The cost is less light: the exposure time must be lengthened by the square of the stop ratio (about 32x in area between f/2.8 and f/16) or the ISO raised, and at slow speeds the image picks up camera shake. There is also a hard limit: a point object is imaged not as a point but as an Airy diffraction pattern of angular radius 1.22 lambda / D, so as the aperture is reduced the diffraction blur grows and eventually overwhelms the gain from the smaller geometric circle of confusion. The optimum is around f/8 to f/11 for a full-frame camera.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Focal length of the human eye</td><td>about 2.3 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Airy disk angular radius at 550 nm (D = 0.1 m)</td><td>about 6.7 microradians (1.22 lambda / D)</td></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of the human cornea</td><td>about 1.376</td></tr><tr><td>Near point of a normal eye</td><td>about 25 cm</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How do observatories image exoplanets that are thousands of light-years away and a few pixels across?</b></p><p class="status">Engineering frontier</p><p>Coronagraphs, adaptive optics and interferometry are combined to suppress starlight by many orders of magnitude, and each step adds its own noise.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Is there a fundamental limit to how small an optical element can be before diffraction dominates?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Abbe's limit sets the classical bound and super-resolution can cross it only in restricted ways; how far this can be pushed for imaging remains an active question.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Two thin lenses of focal length 10 cm and 15 cm are placed 5 cm apart: find the equivalent focal length and locate the image of an object 30 cm from the first lens.</li><li>Design an achromatic doublet from two glass types and explain the trade-off between focal length, spherical aberration and cost.</li><li>Explain why closing the aperture improves depth of field but introduces diffraction blur, and find the aperture that balances the two.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/lenses">Read the chapter notes: Lenses</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-lenses">Lenses 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-calc-optics">Optics lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/webbs-mirrors/">NASA: Webb's Mirrors</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/2-4-thin-lenses">OpenStax: Thin Lenses</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/2-5-the-eye">OpenStax: The Eye</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
